> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: Flowchart of nested `if-else` structure — 3m · `b`: C program to find maximum of three integers — 5m)

### Part (a) — Flowchart of Nested `if-else` Structure

- **Concept:** An outer condition `Condition 1` (`a >= b`) splits control into two branches, each containing an inner decision (`a >= c` on the `Yes` path, `b >= c` on the `No` path):

```ascii
                        +-----------+
                        |   Start   |
                        +-----+-----+
                              |
                              v
                      / Input a, b, c /
                              |
                              v
                         < a >= b? >
                     Yes /         \ No
                        v           v
                 < a >= c? >     < b >= c? >
                Yes /   \ No    Yes /   \ No
                   v     v         v     v
              [max=a] [max=c]   [max=b] [max=c]
                   \      \       /      /
                    +------+-----+------+
                              |
                              v
                        / Print max /
                              |
                              v
                          +-------+
                          |  End  |
                          +-------+
```

- **Branch Summary:**
  - `a >= b` **Yes** and `a >= c` **Yes** → `max = a`; `a >= b` **Yes** and `a >= c` **No** → `max = c`.
  - `a >= b` **No** and `b >= c` **Yes** → `max = b`; `a >= b` **No** and `b >= c` **No** → `max = c`.
  - Every execution path performs **exactly 2 relational comparisons** (`O(1)` time, `O(1)` space), and explicit `{}` braces prevent any dangling-`else` ambiguity.

### Part (b) — C Program to Find the Maximum of Three Integers

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a, b, c, max;

    printf("Enter three integers: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d %d", &a, &b, &c) != 3) return 1;

    if (a >= b) {
        if (a >= c)
            max = a;
        else
            max = c;
    } else {
        if (b >= c)
            max = b;
        else
            max = c;
    }

    printf("Maximum number = %d\n", max);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Run (`input: 12 29 19`):** Outer `12 >= 29` is false (`else` branch) → inner `29 >= 19` is true → `Maximum number = 29`.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: `while` vs `do-while` loop — 3m · `b`: C program to calculate sum of digits — 5m)

### Part (a) — Difference Between `while` and `do-while` Loops

> [!COMPARE] `while` Loop vs `do-while` Loop in C
> | Parameter | `while` Loop | `do-while` Loop |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Loop Classification** | Entry-controlled (top-tested) loop. | Exit-controlled (bottom-tested) loop. |
> | **Condition Evaluation** | Evaluated **before** executing the loop body. | Evaluated **after** executing the loop body. |
> | **Minimum Body Runs** | `0` iterations if the initial condition is false (`0`). | Guaranteed **`1` iteration** even if the condition is initially false. |
> | **Syntax & Semicolon** | `while (cond) { ... }` (no semicolon after header). | `do { ... } while (cond);` (requires trailing semicolon `;`). |

### Part (b) — C Program to Calculate the Sum of Digits of an Integer

- **Digit-Extraction Logic:** Taking `num % 10` extracts the least-significant decimal digit, while integer division `num /= 10` peels that digit off until `num == 0` (handling negative inputs via `num = -num`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    long num, temp;
    int sum = 0;

    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    if (scanf("%ld", &num) != 1) return 1;

    temp = (num < 0) ? -num : num;      /* handle negative numbers */
    while (temp > 0) {
        sum += (int)(temp % 10);        /* add last digit */
        temp /= 10;                     /* drop last digit */
    }

    printf("Sum of digits of %ld = %d\n", num, sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`num = 4079`):**

| Step | `temp` (before) | `temp % 10` | `sum` (after) | `temp / 10` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `1` | `4079` | `9` | `9` | `407` |
| `2` | `407` | `7` | `16` | `40` |
| `3` | `40` | `0` | `16` | `4` |
| `4` | `4` | `4` | `20` | `0` (stop) |

- **Output:** `Sum of digits of 4079 = 20`.

> [!MARKS] 2 + 6 = 8 Marks (`a`: Definition of array — 2m · `b`: C program to read `4x4` matrix and sum diagonal elements — 6m)

### Part (a) — What Is an Array?

- **Definition:** An **array** in C is a derived data structure consisting of a fixed-size, contiguous block of memory locations storing homogeneous elements (all of the **same data type**), accessed in `O(1)` time through a shared array name and zero-based integer indices (`0` to `size - 1`).
- **2D Matrix Representation:** `int mat[4][4];` stores `16` integers (`4` rows × `4` columns) contiguously in **row-major order**.

### Part (b) — C Program to Read a `4x4` Integer Matrix and Sum Its Diagonal Elements

- **Diagonal Condition:** In an `N x N` (`N = 4`) square matrix:
  - **Principal (Main) Diagonal:** Elements `a[i][i]` where `row == col` (`(0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)`).
  - **Secondary (Anti) Diagonal:** Elements `a[i][3 - i]` where `row + col == 3` (`(0,3), (1,2), (2,1), (3,0)` — for even `N = 4`, the two diagonals do not share a center cell).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[4][4], i, j;
    int main_diag = 0, sec_diag = 0;

    printf("Enter 16 integers for the 4x4 matrix:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < 4; j++) {
            if (scanf("%d", &a[i][j]) != 1) return 1;
        }
    }

    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        main_diag += a[i][i];           /* principal diagonal (i == j) */
        sec_diag  += a[i][3 - i];       /* secondary diagonal (i + j == 3) */
    }

    printf("Sum of principal diagonal elements = %d\n", main_diag);
    printf("Sum of secondary diagonal elements = %d\n", sec_diag);
    printf("Total sum of both diagonals = %d\n", main_diag + sec_diag);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Verification:** For the `4x4` identity matrix (`1`s on `a[i][i]`, `0` elsewhere), `main_diag = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4` and `sec_diag = 0`.

> [!MARKS] 2 + 6 = 8 Marks (`a`: Use of `return` statement — 2m · `b`: C program for `sum = 1/1! + 1/2! + ... + 1/n!` — 6m)

### Part (a) — Use of the `return` Statement in C

- **Immediate Control Exit:** Executing `return;` or `return expr;` terminates the currently active function immediately (ignoring any statements written below it in that path) and pops its stack frame to resume the caller.
- **Value Transmission & Exit Status:** In non-`void` functions, `return expr;` converts `expr` to the function's return type and delivers it to the caller; in `main()`, `return 0;` reports successful termination to the operating system.

### Part (b) — C Program to Calculate `sum = 1/1! + 1/2! + 1/3! + ... + 1/n!`

- **Floating-Point & `O(n)` Design:** Maintain `fact` as `double` (`fact *= i`) and compute `1.0 / fact` (using `1.0` rather than integer `1 / fact`, which would truncate `1 / 2!` to `0`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n, i;
    double fact = 1.0, term, sum = 0.0;

    printf("Enter positive integer n: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1) {
        printf("Please enter n >= 1.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        fact *= i;              /* computes i! incrementally */
        term = 1.0 / fact;      /* floating-point division 1 / i! */
        sum += term;            /* accumulate into series sum */
    }

    printf("Sum of series up to %d terms = %.6f\n", n, sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`n = 4`):**

| `i` | `fact` (`i!`) | `term` (`1.0 / i!`) | `sum` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `1` | `1.0` | `1.000000` | `1.000000` |
| `2` | `2.0` | `0.500000` | `1.500000` |
| `3` | `6.0` | `0.166667` | `1.666667` |
| `4` | `24.0` | `0.041667` | `1.708333` |

- **Sample Output (`n = 4`):** `Sum of series up to 4 terms = 1.708333`.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: Recursion concept & example — 3m · `b`: Recursive GCD function & C program — 5m)

### Part (a) — Recursion in C

- **Definition:** **Recursion** is a problem-solving technique in which a function calls itself directly or indirectly on a smaller sub-instance of the problem.
- **Two Essential Components:**
  1. **Base Case (Stopping Condition):** Returns a direct answer without further self-calls (prevents infinite recursion and stack overflow).
  2. **Recursive Case:** Reduces the argument toward the base case (`fact(n) = n * fact(n - 1)`), pushing an activation frame onto the runtime call stack for each active call.

```c
int fact(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;       /* base case */
    return n * fact(n - 1);     /* recursive step */
}
/* fact(4) -> 4 * fact(3) -> 4 * 3 * fact(2) -> 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24 */
```

### Part (b) — Recursive Function to Compute Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)

- **Euclidean Recurrence:** `gcd(a, 0) = a` (base case), and `gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a % b)` when `b != 0` (`O(log(min(a, b)))` recursive calls).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int gcd(int a, int b) {
    if (a < 0) a = -a;
    if (b < 0) b = -b;
    if (b == 0)
        return a;               /* base case */
    return gcd(b, a % b);       /* recursive Euclidean step */
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    printf("Enter two integers: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &x, &y) != 2) return 1;
    printf("GCD of %d and %d = %d\n", x, y, gcd(x, y));
    return 0;
}
```

- **Call-Stack Trace (`gcd(48, 18)`):** `gcd(48, 18) -> gcd(18, 12) -> gcd(12, 6) -> gcd(6, 0) -> returns 6` (`O(log(min(a, b)))` time and call-stack space; also handles `gcd(0, k) = k` cleanly via the first recursive swap).

> [!MARKS] 2 + 6 = 8 Marks (`a`: Meaning of storage class — 2m · `b`: Four C storage classes with summary table & example — 6m)

### Part (a) — Meaning of Storage Class in C

- **Definition:** A **storage class** in C is a declaration specifier alongside the data type that defines four runtime/compile-time properties of a variable: **where** it is stored in memory, its **default initial value**, its **visibility (scope & linkage)**, and its **lifetime (storage duration)**.

### Part (b) — The Four Storage Classes in C

| Storage Class | Storage Location | Default Initial Value | Scope / Visibility | Lifetime |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `auto` | Stack (RAM) | Garbage (indeterminate) | Local to enclosing `{}` block | Created on block entry; destroyed on block exit |
| `register` | CPU register (or stack fallback) | Garbage (indeterminate) | Local to enclosing `{}` block | Block duration (`&var` address-of forbidden) |
| `static` | Data / BSS segment | `0` | Local to block, or file-internal if global | Persists for the entire program execution |
| `extern` | Data / BSS segment | `0` | Global across multiple `.c` source files | Persists for the entire program execution |

- **Detailed Behaviour & Program Example:**
  - **`auto`:** Implicit default for all local variables (`auto int x = 5;` is identical to `int x = 5;`).
  - **`register`:** Advises the compiler to keep heavily accessed loop counters in fast CPU registers (`register int i;`).
  - **`static`:** A local `static` variable is initialized **only once** and retains its updated value between calls; a global `static` identifier is private to its own `.c` file.
  - **`extern`:** Declares that a global variable (`extern int total;`) is defined in this or another translation unit without allocating duplicate storage.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int global_val = 100;               /* external linkage */

void demo(void) {
    auto int a = 1;                 /* re-initialized every call */
    static int s = 1;               /* retains value across calls */
    printf("a=%d, s=%d\n", a++, s++);
}

int main(void) {
    extern int global_val;
    register int i;
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) demo();
    printf("global_val=%d\n", global_val);
    return 0;
}
/* Output: a=1, s=1 / a=1, s=2 / a=1, s=3 / global_val=100 */
```

> [!MARKS] 2 + 6 = 8 Marks (`a`: Definition of pointer — 2m · `b`: Call-by-value vs call-by-address with C swap program — 6m)

### Part (a) — What Is a Pointer?

- **Definition:** A **pointer** in C is a derived variable that stores the **memory address** of another typed variable or function rather than a direct data value.
- **Core Operators:** `&x` (address-of) yields the byte address of `x`; `*p` (dereference / indirection) accesses the value stored at address `p` (`int x = 10, *p = &x;` ⇒ `*p == 10`).

### Part (b) — Call-by-Value vs Call-by-Address Parameter Passing

> [!COMPARE] Call-by-Value vs Call-by-Address in C
> | Parameter | Call-by-Value | Call-by-Address (Pointer Passing) |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Argument Passed** | Copy of the actual variable's **value** (`swapVal(a, b)`). | Memory **address** of the actual variable (`swapAddr(&a, &b)`). |
> | **Formal Parameter** | Ordinary local variable (`int x, int y`). | Pointer variable (`int *x, int *y`). |
> | **Effect on Caller** | Modifying `x` or `y` alters only the local copy; caller variables stay unchanged. | Dereferencing `*x` and `*y` mutates the caller's original variables in place. |
> | **Typical Use Case** | Read-only scalar inputs (`sqrt(n)`, `fact(n)`). | Swapping values, returning multiple results, passing arrays/structures. |

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swapVal(int x, int y) {
    int t = x; x = y; y = t;        /* swaps local copies only */
}

void swapAddr(int *x, int *y) {
    int t = *x; *x = *y; *y = t;    /* swaps caller variables via addresses */
}

int main(void) {
    int a = 10, b = 20;
    swapVal(a, b);
    printf("After swapVal:  a=%d, b=%d\n", a, b);   /* a=10, b=20 */
    swapAddr(&a, &b);
    printf("After swapAddr: a=%d, b=%d\n", a, b);   /* a=20, b=10 */
    return 0;
}
```
